    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "During the 1950s, policymakers and segregationists relied on flawed sociological and pseudo-scientific theories to justify their arguments. One prominent example is the idea of the \"melting pot\" vs. the \"plastic people\" debate, popularized by sterilization advocates and eugenicists. They claimed that racial mixing would reduce intellectual and cultural achievement, leading to a \"dilution\" of the white American population's perceived superior qualities. Another theory cited was the concept of \"integration anxiety,\" which posited that close proximity between individuals of different racial groups would lead to anxiety and tension, ultimately deteriorating social cohesion.\n\nThe \" contre Attila\" analogy also played a role, where some argued that America was threatened by an influx of \"ours\" (whites from Europe, especially Eastern Europeans and Slavs), who were seen as bringing inferior cultural values and sparking nationalist threats. These pseudo-scientific theories were often used in conjunction with concerns about moral decay, fear of \"outbreeding,\" and alleged damage to societal norms, to justify segregation as a means of social preservation.",
